Original commit body from e9213d0c2bc3d89b6d1d8e229ae3e7b718ca8f7d:

cfg_nodes/typescript.yaml has asserted in its own header since it landed that
it 'also covers JavaScript (same node types in tree-sitter-javascript)'. But
the mapping, the def/use extractor and the LanguageDdgSpec were all keyed to
'typescript' and the spec's glob was '*.ts', so a .js file reached none of it.

JavaScript is the half of that pair whose edges can move a taint verdict: 50
catalogued sources and 83 sinks, against TypeScript's 6 and zero.

Three keys, no new logic: a second @register_def_use_extractor('javascript')
on the existing class, a second LanguageDdgSpec with a '*.js' glob, and one
entry in a new cfg._CFG_MAPPING_ALIASES. The alias encodes the shared-grammar
assertion once instead of copying its consequences into a javascript.yaml free
to drift from the original.

Measured on a fixture: the http.createServer -> fs.writeFileSync flow in the
recall corpus moves from approximate/structural to precise/ddg. That is the
walk running on JavaScript and confirming a data dependence. It is a FIXTURE,
not a cohort - no claim is made here about express or apollo-server.

register_def_use_extractor now stamps 'language' on each registration, so
stacking two decorators no longer leaves the second instance carrying the
first's name. No-op for every existing single registration.

test_cfg_mapping_declares_atomic_statement re-derived the mapping path itself
rather than asking production's loader - a second implementation of 'where
does a language's mapping live' that broke the moment a mapping legitimately
lived under another key. It now calls load_cfg_mapping.

Wiring a language invalidated three tests that used it as their INCAPABLE
exemplar. Flipping their assertions to True would have left tests that
distinguish nothing, so Java takes the role: it has a cfg mapping but no
atomic_statement and no extractor, which also exercises the partial-blockers
case for the first time. Its fixture contributes zero evidence rows, verified
rather than assumed.

One test lost coverage that could not be replaced, and says so in place.
test_adjudication_label_does_not_depend_on_another_language drew its power
from the flow's own language being incapable; JavaScript was the only language
that both produced a fixture flow and lacked an extractor (java, c, php and
ruby were each tried and yield zero evidence rows). The invariant stays pinned
at unit level by test_function_the_ddg_never_saw_is_structural_not_mixed,
which builds the shape synthetically and cannot be invalidated by wiring a
language. What is gone is the end-to-end confirmation.

Two filing claims corrected rather than propagated: 'JavaScript is the largest
unwired taint surface' is false (fifth by sink count, behind elixir 301,
erlang 157, haskell 131, kotlin 93); and an in-file apollo-server figure of
588 edges / 93 symbols was measured on an older tree and is marked for
re-measurement. The '*.js' glob deliberately does not claim .mjs/.cjs/.jsx.

